<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-briefings/">Briefings</a></li><li>The One-Year Clock: Ohio Suit-Limitation Clauses During Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Briefing · 7 min read</p><h1>The One-Year Clock: Ohio Suit-Limitation Clauses During Appraisal</h1><p class="lede">Appraisal can take months, and many Ohio policies give you only a year to sue. Knowing how those timelines overlap protects your options.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Ohio Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-10-21">October 21, 2026</time> · 7 min read</p>
<h2>A deadline hiding in the conditions section</h2>
<p>Most Ohio homeowners never notice the paragraph titled something like “Suit Against Us.” It usually says no action can be brought unless the policy terms were followed and the action is started within a set time after the loss — often one year, sometimes two. Ohio courts have generally enforced these contractual deadlines, even though they are much shorter than the general statute of limitations for written contracts. That makes the Ohio suit limitation clause one of the most important dates in any disputed claim.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">We are not attorneys</b>This article explains the general landscape so you can ask better questions. Whether your deadline has been extended, tolled or waived is a legal question. Check your policy and talk to an Ohio attorney.</div>

<h2>Why appraisal and the clock collide</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a contract procedure, not a lawsuit. Depending on how fast each side names appraisers, whether an umpire is needed and how long the insurer takes to pay, a residential appraisal can run several months. A disputed claim often reaches appraisal only after weeks or months of inspections and supplements. Add it together and a year disappears quickly.</p>
<p>Many policyholders assume that once appraisal starts, the suit deadline pauses. Some policies or circumstances may support that; many do not say so. If something goes wrong after the award — the insurer disputes coverage for part of it, withholds depreciation or delays payment — you may need to file suit, and an expired deadline can end that option.</p>

<p>Some policies also tie the suit deadline to other conditions, such as cooperating with the investigation, submitting a proof of loss or completing appraisal. Read the whole conditions section, not just the paragraph with the time limit, because each requirement can affect whether a later lawsuit is allowed.</p>
<h2>Where the time goes</h2>
<p>A rough, illustrative timeline for a spring hail claim shows how a year fills up:</p>
<ol>
<li>Date of loss in late May; claim reported within days.</li>
<li>Inspection and first estimate four to six weeks later.</li>
<li>Supplement requests and re-inspection through late summer.</li>
<li>Appraisal demand in early fall; appraisers named within about 20 days.</li>
<li>Inspections, estimate exchange and umpire selection through late fall.</li>
<li>Umpire decision and signed award around the turn of the year.</li>
<li>Payment, then depreciation release after repairs in spring.</li>
</ol>
<p>Every step in that example is ordinary, and the last one lands near the one-year mark.</p>

<h2>Practical steps to protect your options</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Find the clause now.</strong> Read the suit-limitation paragraph and any endorsements that change it. Note whether it runs from the date of loss or another event.</li>
<li><strong>Put the date on a calendar.</strong> Add reminders at 90, 60 and 30 days before it. The <a href="/appraisal-workbench/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a> does this alongside appraisal milestones.</li>
<li><strong>Do not wait for the award to think about it.</strong> If the deadline is within a few months and the claim is still unresolved, that is the time to consult an Ohio attorney.</li>
<li><strong>Get any extension in writing.</strong> If the insurer agrees to extend the deadline, a clear written agreement is far better than an adjuster’s phone assurance.</li>
<li><strong>Keep moving.</strong> Respond to requests promptly and name your appraiser on time so delay is not on your side of the ledger.</li>
</ul>

<p>Also keep copies of everything. Emails, letters, estimates and notes of phone calls with dates and names all help an attorney evaluate your position if a deadline question comes up. A one-page timeline of the claim, updated as you go, is worth the few minutes it takes.</p>
<h2>How this connects to claim-handling rules</h2>
<p>The Ohio Department of Insurance’s unfair claims settlement practices rule, Ohio Administrative Code 3901-1-54, sets standards for how insurers acknowledge, investigate and pay claims, including communications about time limits. Ohio has no statute giving policyholders a private bad-faith claim, but Ohio courts recognize a common-law bad-faith claim when an insurer lacks reasonable justification for how it handles or pays a claim. Whether any of that bears on a missed deadline is, again, a question for a lawyer.</p>

<p>None of this means appraisal is risky. It is a routine, efficient way to resolve price disputes. It simply means the Ohio suit limitation clause keeps running in the background, and someone needs to be watching it.</p>
<h2>Where an appraiser fits</h2>
<p>An appraiser’s job is to value the loss. A good one will also keep the process moving and flag when the calendar looks tight. If you need help starting appraisal quickly, our <a href="/appraisal-practice/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> service prepares and sends the demand and tracks the selection deadlines. For terms like “suit limitation” and “proof of loss,” see the <a href="/appraisal-lexicon/">appraisal lexicon</a>.</p>
